WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.328
Context-aware output encoding means handling data according to the browser
context where it will be placed.

2
00:00:06.328 --> 00:00:13.274
HTML body text, attribute values, URLs, JavaScript strings, JSON
responses, and CSS each need appropriate treatment.

3
00:00:13.274 --> 00:00:18.639
A safe Python application does not ask whether data has been escaped in a
vague general sense.

4
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:23.454
It asks whether the data has been handled correctly for this exact output
location.

5
00:00:23.454 --> 00:00:31.501
Template autoescaping helps reduce risk in common HTML contexts,
especially when developers use standard variable insertion patterns.

6
00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:34.527
But autoescaping is not a license to ignore context.

7
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:42.299
Attributes, URLs, script-adjacent data, and client-side use can require
additional constraints or different rendering patterns.

8
00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:49.521
Developers should avoid raw or unsafe rendering shortcuts unless the
content has a reviewed reason and a controlled policy.

9
00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:58.394
Input validation alone does not reliably prevent XSS because validation
usually happens before the application knows every future rendering
context.

10
00:00:58.394 --> 00:01:04.515
A display name may pass a length check and character policy, yet still
need encoding when placed into HTML.

11
00:01:04.515 --> 00:01:09.674
A search term may be valid input, yet still require safe handling when
reflected in a page.

12
00:01:09.674 --> 00:01:14.144
Validation is useful, but output safety is decided at the point of
rendering.

13
00:01:14.144 --> 00:01:17.033
JSON response safety deserves its own attention.

14
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:21.435
Returning data as JSON does not automatically make every frontend use
safe.

15
00:01:21.435 --> 00:01:27.831
Browser code may insert values into text nodes, attributes, URLs,
templates, or markup-building functions.

16
00:01:27.831 --> 00:01:36.360
Backend and frontend teams should agree on data contracts and safe
rendering practices so API data remains data throughout the path to the
browser.
